WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.040
When you open a Linux terminal and
type a command, like ls > out.txt,

2
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.100
you start a very specific chain reaction.

3
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.260
To understand how this works, we
first need to define a process.

4
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.130
A process is a running program,
together with its context.

5
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.800
This context includes its memory, its CPU
registers, and its open file descriptors.

6
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.660
This diagram shows the OS architecture.

7
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.750
The shell operates in user mode,
above the privilege boundary.

8
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.250
Below is kernel mode. To run a command,
the shell crosses down to issue the fork

9
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.290
system call, creating a child process, an
exact clone sharing the same memory layout

10
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.180
and open file descriptors.

11
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.840
Cloning the existing shell is the
mandatory first step in Linux.

12
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.830
It delegates the new work to a subordinate
process, while the original shell stays

13
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.220
running, ready to accept
your next command.

14
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.950
We can now shift our focus
entirely to the child process.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.350
Before it actually runs the list
command, it has to handle the > out.txt

16
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:09.280
redirection. This animated diagram shows
how the child manages that text flow.

17
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.710
By issuing the dup2 system call, the child
process modifies its own context, routing

18
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.940
the output file descriptor
directly to the text file.

19
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.700
Next comes a crucial transformation
using the execve system call.

20
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.270
The child process swaps out the copied shell
code and loads the new ls program code.

21
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.890
Notice that the file descriptor pointing
to the text file remains exactly where it

22
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.310
was. Because execve keeps those file
descriptors intact, the ls program writes to the

23
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.220
text file automatically. It never needs
to know its output was redirected.

24
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.810
With the code loaded, the
process needs CPU time.

25
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.990
It will cycle through three states, ready,
waiting for the CPU, running, actively

26
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.130
executing, and blocked if it
has to wait for disk I/O.

27
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:58.020
To share the CPU, the operating
system performs a context switch.

28
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.830
It saves the registers of the currently
running process and loads the saved

29
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.710
registers of the next one in line.

30
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.900
The scheduler decides whose turn it is.

31
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.950
On Linux, since version 6.6, the kernel
uses the EEVDF algorithm, which shares the

32
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.640
CPU by allocating virtual time,
weighted by each process's priority.

33
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.930
Execve ensures your program runs with the
correct file setup, and the scheduler's

34
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.100
rapid context switches
let it share the CPU.

35
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.550
This allows your redirected command to
execute smoothly alongside the rest of the

36
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.750
operating system. Eventually, the ls command
finishes reading the directory and ends

37
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.460
its execution. But the process
doesn't vanish completely.

38
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.270
Its process control block,
or PCB, stays in memory.

39
00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.060
The process is now a zombie.

40
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.170
The PCB stays intact so the parent
shell can issue the wait system call.

41
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.920
Once it collects the final exit status,
the kernel removes the zombie PCB.

42
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.720
That exit status provides exact
details of the termination.

43
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.370
It tells the parent whether the program
exited cleanly or if it crashed, perhaps due

44
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.380
to a page fault triggering a signal.

45
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.040
In this terminal output, you can
see an example of crash data.

46
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.690
An exit status of 139 indicates the process
was killed by a segmentation fault, or

47
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.320
SIGSEGV, which is signal 11 added
to the shell convention of 128.

48
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.340
Every command you run follows
this strict chronological cycle.

49
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.190
It clones an existing process, transforms
its code, shares the CPU through the

50
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.340
scheduler, and ends by reporting
its status to the kernel.

51
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.250
Let's review. Which system call replaces
the process's program while keeping its

52
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.480
file descriptors?

53
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.290
execve. What is the state of a process
that has ended but its parent has not yet

54
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.300
called wait?

55
00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:53.720
Zombie. Now open animations A4.3 and
A4.4 in the course hub, and run lab TD3.
